#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:4
msgid "Skipped binaries"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:6
msgid ""
"Conan always computes the complete dependency graph: every recipe of every "
"dependency is resolved, loaded and evaluated, and every node gets its "
"``package_id`` computed. But computing the graph doesn't mean that all the "
"package binaries in that graph are necessary. Very often, a large part of "
"the dependencies binaries are not needed at all for the current command, and "
"Conan avoids downloading, building or installing them. These binaries are "
"marked as **skipped** (``Skip``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:12
msgid ""
"Skipping binaries is the default behavior, it is a deliberate optimization "
"that can save a lot of download time, disk space and build time, and it is "
"also one of the reasons why the graph :ref:`package types and requirement "
"traits<reference_conanfile_methods_requirements>` are so important."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:16
msgid ""
"This section explains when and why Conan skips binaries, how to recognize it "
"and how to change this behavior when needed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:21
msgid "Recognizing skipped binaries"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:23
msgid "Let's use the following dependency graph as an example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:25
msgid "``app/0.1`` is an ``application`` that ``requires(\"openssl/0.1\")``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:26
msgid "``openssl/0.1`` is a ``shared-library`` that ``requires(\"zlib/0.1\")``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:27
msgid ""
"``zlib/0.1`` is a ``static-library`` that has a ``tool_requires(\"tool/"
"0.1\")`` (some build tool)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:29
msgid ""
"Installing the ``app/0.1`` application, assuming that all the binaries have "
"already been created:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:31
msgid ""
"$ conan install --requires=app/0.1\n"
"...\n"
"======== Computing necessary packages ========\n"
"Requirements\n"
"    app/0.1#f285...:cba9...#c921... - Cache\n"
"    openssl/0.1#b66c...:43fa...#0789... - Cache\n"
"Build requirements\n"
"Skipped host binaries\n"
"    zlib/0.1\n"
"Skipped build binaries\n"
"    tool/0.1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:46
msgid ""
"The \"Computing necessary packages\" step lists the binaries that are "
"actually going to be used, and then, in separate ``Skipped host binaries``, "
"``Skipped test binaries`` and ``Skipped build binaries`` sections, the "
"packages whose binaries are not necessary. In this case:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:50
msgid ""
"The ``zlib`` static library has been linked into the ``openssl`` shared "
"library. Using ``openssl`` doesn't require the ``zlib`` binary at all, "
"neither to build ``app`` nor to run it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:52
msgid ""
"The ``tool/0.1`` was only necessary to build ``zlib``. As ``zlib`` is not "
"being built from source, there is no need for the ``tool/0.1`` binary at all."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:55
msgid "Skipped binaries are also visible in other places:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:57
msgid ""
"``conan graph info`` shows ``binary: Skip`` for those nodes, and in "
"its :ref:`json output<reference_commands_graph_info_json_format>` the "
"``\"binary\"`` field of the node will be ``\"Skip\"``. The requirement edges "
"in the json output also contain a ``\"skip\"`` field."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:60
msgid ""
"In recipes, generators and deployers, the dependencies whose binaries are "
"skipped still exist in ``self.dependencies``, but their ``package_folder`` "
"is ``None`` (see :ref:`the dependencies "
"reference<reference_conanfile_methods_generate>`). Their ``package_info()`` "
"method is not evaluated either."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:64
msgid "``conan graph build-order`` doesn't include skipped binaries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:68
msgid "When is a binary necessary?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:70
msgid ""
"Conan computes which binaries are necessary starting from the consumer (the "
"``conanfile`` being installed, or the ``--requires`` given in the command "
"line) and from the packages that are going to be built from source, and "
"following the requirement traits (``headers``, ``libs``, ``run``, ``build``) "
"that are propagated through the graph. The rules are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:75
msgid ""
"The **direct dependencies of the consumer** are always necessary, including "
"its ``tool_requires`` and ``test_requires``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:77
msgid ""
"A **package that is going to be built from source** needs the binaries of "
"all its direct dependencies, including its ``tool_requires`` and "
"``test_requires``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:79
msgid ""
"A **transitive dependency** binary is necessary only if some of its "
"\"files\" are needed by a node that needs it, that is, if the requirement "
"propagated to that node through the graph still has some of the ``headers``, "
"``libs``, ``run`` or ``build`` traits enabled. If all of them have been "
"dropped along the way, the transitive dependency binary is skipped."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:83
msgid ""
"Any binary that is not necessary according to the above rules is skipped."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:85
msgid ""
"The propagation of traits is derived from the ``package_type`` of the "
"packages, and it follows the same \"embed\" and \"non-embed\" cases "
"explained in :ref:`the effect of dependencies on "
"package_id<reference_binary_model_dependencies>`. For a ``consumer`` that "
"depends on a ``direct-dep`` package, which in turn depends on a ``transitive-"
"dep`` package:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:90
msgid "consumer -> direct-dep -> transitive-dep"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:94
msgid ""
"**Embed mode**: if ``direct-dep`` embeds ``transitive-dep`` (for example, a "
"shared library or an application linking a static library, or a static "
"library compiling a header-only library), the ``transitive-dep`` artifacts "
"are already contained inside the ``direct-dep`` binary, so the ``transitive-"
"dep`` binary is skipped. This is the same reason why ``direct-dep`` needs a "
"new ``package_id`` for every change of ``transitive-dep`` (``full_mode`` by "
"default)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:99
msgid ""
"**Non-embed mode**: if ``direct-dep`` doesn't embed ``transitive-dep`` (for "
"example, any library linking a shared library, or a static library linking "
"another static library), the ``consumer`` will still need the ``transitive-"
"dep`` binary, to link it or to run it, so it is not skipped."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:102
msgid ""
"If ``direct-dep`` is a **header-only** library, it doesn't embed anything, "
"so the ``transitive-dep`` binary is necessary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:104
msgid ""
"The ``tool_requires`` and ``test_requires`` of ``direct-dep`` and "
"``transitive-dep`` are only necessary if ``direct-dep`` or ``transitive-"
"dep`` respectively are going to be built from source. Otherwise, they are "
"skipped."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:107
msgid ""
"The traits can be modified explicitly in the ``requires()`` calls, like "
"``transitive_headers=True`` or ``transitive_libs=True``, and that will also "
"change which binaries are necessary. See the :ref:`requirement traits "
"reference<reference_conanfile_methods_requirements>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:113
msgid ""
"The correct definition of ``package_type`` (explicitly, or implicitly via "
"the ``shared`` option) is critical for this behavior. If the package types "
"are unknown, Conan cannot know if a dependency has been embedded or not, and "
"will be conservative, propagating the traits and requiring more binaries "
"than necessary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:120
msgid "Skipped binaries and build modes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:122
msgid ""
"The skipping of binaries happens after Conan has decided which binaries "
"exist and which need to be built, and it has some implications that are "
"worth knowing:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:125
msgid ""
"**A missing binary that is skipped is not an error**. In the example above, "
"if the ``zlib/0.1`` binary doesn't exist in the cache or in any remote, "
"``conan install --requires=app/0.1`` will still succeed, because that binary "
"is not needed. Only the binaries that are necessary will raise a \"missing "
"binary\" error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:128
msgid ""
"**Build missing only builds necessary binaries**. If the ``zlib/0.1`` binary "
"is missing, but ``app`` and ``openssl`` binaries exist, ``conan install --"
"requires=app/0.1 --build=missing`` will not build ``zlib`` from source, it "
"will be skipped. The same applies to ``--build=missing:[pattern]``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:131
msgid ""
"**Forced builds always build**. Packages explicitly forced to be built with "
"``--build=\"pkg/*\"`` (or ``--build=\"*\"``) are always built, even if their "
"binaries wouldn't be necessary otherwise. As they are built, their "
"dependencies binaries become necessary too. In the example above, ``conan "
"install --requires=app/0.1 --build=\"openssl/*\" --build=missing`` will "
"build ``openssl``, and as it needs ``zlib`` to link, ``zlib`` will also be "
"built if its binary is missing, and ``tool/0.1`` will no longer be skipped, "
"as it is needed to build ``zlib``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:138
msgid ""
"$ conan install --requires=app/0.1 --build=\"openssl/*\" --build=missing\n"
"...\n"
"Requirements\n"
"    app/0.1#f285...:cba9...#c921... - Cache\n"
"    openssl/0.1#b66c...:43fa... - Build\n"
"    zlib/0.1#f6a2...:dcf6... - Build\n"
"Build requirements\n"
"    tool/0.1#6cb7...:dcf6...#e018... - Cache"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:150
msgid ""
"Note that the recipes of skipped packages are still necessary: they are "
"downloaded (if not in the cache) and evaluated to compute the dependency "
"graph and the ``package_id`` of their consumers. Skipping is only about the "
"package binaries. Skipped packages are still part of the dependency graph, "
"so they will also be captured in lockfiles, and listed by ``conan graph "
"info``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:155
msgid ""
"The ``tools.graph:skip_build`` and ``tools.graph:skip_test`` confs are a "
"different thing: they make Conan not expand ``tool_requires`` and "
"``test_requires`` at all, so not even their recipes are part of the graph. "
"In most cases they are not necessary, as Conan will already skip those "
"binaries when they are not needed. See :ref:`the build_requirements() "
"reference<reference_conanfile_methods_build_requirements>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:162
msgid "Disabling the skipping of binaries"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:164
msgid ""
"The default behavior should be good for the vast majority of cases, but "
"there are some situations in which all the binaries of the graph are needed, "
"for example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:167
msgid ""
"Deploying all the dependencies binaries with "
"a :ref:`deployer<reference_extensions_deployers>`, including the ones that "
"have been embedded in other binaries."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:169
msgid ""
"Collecting information that is only available in the package binaries of the "
"whole graph, like licenses files."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:170
msgid ""
"Populating a cache or a server with all the binaries of a dependency graph, "
"or building all of them."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:172
msgid "There are a few mechanisms to modify the default behavior:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:174
msgid ""
"The ``tools.graph:skip_binaries`` conf, ``True`` by default. Defining it as "
"``False`` will make all binaries necessary:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:177
msgid ""
"# Only the \"host\" context binaries, \"tool_requires\" binaries can still "
"be skipped\n"
"$ conan install --requires=app/0.1 -c tools.graph:skip_binaries=False\n"
"# All binaries, both in the \"host\" and \"build\" contexts\n"
"$ conan install --requires=app/0.1 -c:a tools.graph:skip_binaries=False"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:186
msgid ""
"The ``-c`` argument only applies to the \"host\" context. To also avoid "
"skipping the binaries of the \"build\" context, like the ``tool_requires`` "
"and their dependencies, use ``-c:a`` (all contexts) or ``-c:b``, or define "
"it in the ``[conf]`` section of the build profile."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:190
msgid ""
"It can also be defined per-package using :ref:`conf "
"patterns<reference_config_files_global_conf_patterns>`, to avoid skipping "
"only some specific packages binaries, like ``-c \"zlib/"
"*:tools.graph:skip_binaries=False\"``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:193
msgid ""
"If a package that would have been built only because of ``--build=missing`` "
"defines ``tools.graph:skip_binaries=False``, it will be built (and its "
"dependencies binaries will be necessary), even if its binary would have been "
"skipped otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:197
msgid ""
"The :ref:`no_skip=True requirement "
"trait<reference_conanfile_methods_requirements_no_skip>`, defined in a "
"recipe ``requires()``, forces the dependency binary to be necessary whenever "
"the consumer binary is necessary. This should only be used in exceptional "
"cases."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../reference/binary_model/skipped_binaries.rst:201
msgid ""
"If a dependency always needs to be available at runtime for all of its "
"consumers, for example a ``static-library`` package that also contains some "
"shared libraries, "
"the :ref:`package_type_traits<conan_conanfile_attributes_package_type_traits>` "
"attribute can be used in that dependency recipe to define ``\"run\": True``, "
"so the ``run`` trait is propagated downstream and its binary will not be "
"skipped."
msgstr ""
